      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given that \\(a\\) varies inversely with \\(b^2\\), we can express this relationship as:\n\\[ a = \\frac{k}{b^2} \\]\nfor some constant \\(k\\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the constant \\(k\\) using the given values \\(a = 9\\) when \\(b = 2\\). Substituting these values into the equation, we get:\n\\[ 9 = \\frac{k}{2^2} \\]\n\\[ 9 = \\frac{k}{4} \\]\nTo solve for \\(k\\), we multiply both sides by 4:\n\\[ k = 9 \\times 4 = 36 \\]\n\nNow that we have the constant \\(k\\), we can use it to find the value of \\(a\\) when \\(b = 3\\). Substituting \\(b = 3\\) and \\(k = 36\\) into the equation \\(a = \\frac{k}{b^2}\\), we get:\n\\[ a = \\frac{36}{3^2} \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{36}{9} \\]\n\\[ a = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) when \\(b = 3\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
